Experiment no: 03
Aim:- To design and implement a lexical analyzer
that reads a source program and identifies
token such as keywords, identifiers, operators
literals and seperators and reports lexical
errors if any.
Theory:
lexical Analyzer
A lexical Analyzer is the first phase of compiler
It reads the source program character by character,
group characters into meaningful sequence
called lexemes, and produce tokens as output.
Token
is a pair of
1. token name   2) Attribute value (optional)
Eg:- int a = 10;
lexeme             Token type
int                keyword
a                  identifier
=                  operator
10                 number
;                  seperator
Type of tokens
1. keywords - reserved words (int, float, etc)
2. Identifiers - name of variable.
3. Operators - +, -, *, /, =, <, >
4. Seperators - ;, (), {}
5. literals - numbers & constants
6. comments - ignored by the compiler
Algorithm:
1. Read the source program input
2. Remove the extra comments & extra spaces
3. Scan characters from right to left.
4. Identify lexeme based on predefined rules
5. classify each lexeme into its token type.
6. Display the token.
7. Report invalid or unknown symbols or errors.
Conclusion:
The lexical analyzer plays an vital role in compiler
design by converting a stream of characters into
token. this experiment helped in understanding
token identification, lexical rule and error handling
