# lexical_analyzer.py 
import re 
code = "int a = b + 10;" 
keywords = ["int", "float", "if", "else"] 
tokens = re.findall(r"[A-Za-z_]\w*|\d+|[=+*/;]", code) 
for token in tokens: 
if token in keywords: 
print(f"Keyword: {token}") 
elif token.isdigit(): 
print(f"Number: {token}") 
elif token.isidentifier(): 
print(f"Identifier: {token}") 
else: 
print(f"Operator/Symbol: {token}")

exp. No: 4
Title: implementation of intermediate code generation phase.
Aim: To design and Implement the intermediate code generation
(ICG) phase of a compiler that converts source code
into an intermediate representation such as Three address
code (TAC).
Theory:
Intermediate Code Generation
It is the third phase of a compiler which comes after
lexical analysis & syntax analysis this phase converts
the parse tree or syntax tree into a intermediate
representation (IR) that's easy to optimize &
translate in to machine code.
Need for Intermediate code
• make compiler machine independent
• Simpler code optimization
• Make target code generation easier.
Types of statements in ICG:
• assignment statements
• Arithmetic statements
• Conditional statements
• Looping construct
Algorithm
1. Read the input expression or source program
2. Parse the input and generate syntax tree
3. Identify operator and operands
4. Generate temporary variable
5. Produce three address code instruction
6. Display the generated intermediate code
Advantage of intermediate code
• Improve portability of compiler
• simplify optimization
Conclusion
The intermediate code generation phase act as a
bridge the front end and backend of the compiler. The
experiment demonstrate how complex expressions
can be broken into simpler instructions using temporary
variable.
Code :=
class IntermediateCodeGenerator:
    def __init__(self):
        self.temp_count = 0
        self.label_count = 0
    def generate_temp(self):
        temp_name = f"t{self.temp_count}"
        self.temp_count += 1
        return temp_name
    def generate_label(self):
        label_name = f"L{self.label_count}"
        self.label_count += 1
        return label_name
    def generate_code(self, op, arg1="", arg2="", result=""):
        if op == "goto":
            return f"goto {arg1}"
        elif op == "=":
            return f"{result} = {arg1}"
        else:
            return f"{result} = {arg1} {op} {arg2}"
# Example usag
generator = IntermediateCodeGenerator()
# Generate temporary variables and labels
temp1 = generator.generate_temp()
temp2 = generator.generate_temp()
label1 = generator.generate_label()
# Generate intermediate code
code1 = generator.generate_code('+', 'x', 'y', temp1)
code2 = generator.generate_code('*', temp1, 'z', temp2)
code3 = generator.generate_code('-', temp2, 'w', 'result')
code4 = generator.generate_code('goto', label1)
# Print generated code
print(code1)
print(code2)
print(code3)
print(code4)

Exp. No: 5
Title: Implementation of code generation phase of compiler
Aim: To design and implement the code generation phase of
a compiler that converts intermediate code into target
machine code/assembly code.
Theory:
Code generation phase
The code generation phase is the final phase of a
compiler it takes the intermediate representation (IR)
produces by the earlier phases & translates it into
target code, usually assembly or machine code.
Inputs to code generator
• Intermediate code (three address code)
• Symbol Table
• Register & memory information
Outputs of code generator
• Assembly language code
• machine code (in real compiler)
Tasks performed by code generator
1. Instruction selection
2. Register allocation
3. Address calculation
4. Target code generation
Example:
Intermediate Code (TAC)
t1 = b * c
t2 = a + t1
x = t2
generated assembly code:
MOV R1, b
MUL R1, c
MOV R2, a
ADD R2, R1
MOV x, R2
Algorithm:
1. Read the intermediate code as input
2. Analyze each instruction
3. Select appropriate target machine instruction
4. Allocate register to variable and temporary
5. Generate assembly code generation
6. Output the target code
Advantage
• Produce executable code
• optimize resource usage
Conclusion:
The code generation phase bridge the gap between
intermediate representation and executable command. This
experiment demonstrate instruction selection, register allocation
& target code generation.
Code:
class CodeGenerator:
    def __init__(self):
        self.instructions = []

    def emit(self, opcode, operand=None):
        if operand is not None:
            self.instructions.append((opcode, operand))
        else:
            self.instructions.append((opcode,))

    def generate_code(self, node):
        if node['type'] == 'BINARY_OP':
            self.generate_code(node['left'])
            self.generate_code(node['right'])
            self.emit('BINARY_' + node['operator'])

        elif node['type'] == 'NUMBER':
            self.emit('PUSH', node['value'])
